      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find out how many books Melvin will have after repacking the boxes into sets containing 40 books each.\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of books:**\n\n   We start by calculating the total number of books:\n   \\[\n   1335 \\text{ boxes} \\times 39 \\text{ books per box} = 52065 \\text{ books}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine how many complete sets of 40 books each Melvin can make:**\n\n   We need to find out how many complete sets of 40 books can be made from 52065 books:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{52065 \\text{ books}}{40 \\text{ books per set}} = 1301.625\n   \\]\n   \n   Since we cannot have a fraction of a set of books, we take the integer part of the division:\n   \\[\n   1301 \\text{ full sets of 40 books} = 1301 \\times 40 = 52040 \\text{ books in 1301 sets}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the number of books left over:**\n\n   To find out how many books are left over, we subtract the number of books in the complete sets from the total number of books:\n   \\[\n   52065 \\text{ books} - 52040 \\text{ books} = 25 \\text{ books left over}\n   \\]\n\nThus, after repacking as many boxes as possible with 40 books each, Melvin has \\boxed{25} books left over.",
