      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine how many books Melvin has left over after repackaging them into boxes with 40 books each, we follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of books:**\n   Since there are 1335 boxes, each containing 39 books, the total number of books is:\n   \\[\n   1335 \\times 39\n   \\]\n\n2. **Perform the multiplication:**\n   First, we count the total number of books:\n   \\[\n   1335 \\times 39\n   \\]\n   Breaking it down for easier calculation:\n   \\[\n   1335 \\times 39 = 1335 \\times (40 - 1) = 1335 \\times 40 - 1335 \\times 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   1335 \\times 40 = 53400\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   1335 \\times 1 = 1335\n   \\]\n   Subtracting the two:\n   \\[\n   53400 - 1335 = 52065\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the number of books left over:**\n   To find out how many books are left after repacking:\n   \\[\n   52065 \\text{ books repacked into groups of 40}\n   \\]\n   We perform:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total repacked books} = \\frac{52065}{40}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   1301 \\text{ books (complete repackages of 40 each)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Remaining books} = 52065 \\mod 40\n   \\]\n   Calculating modulus:\n   \\[\n   52065 \\mod 40 = 65\n   \\]\n\n4. **Final answer:**\n   Therefore, Melvin has \\( \\boxed{65} \\) books left over after repacking.",
